{"kind":"assert","text":"Measured the cost of the dependency this request is sequenced behind, and it is larger than the sequencing note suggests.\n\nCounted against the live workroom just now: 93 artifacts claim path dot, 55 of them live and 38 retired. The fold reports 151 omitted supersessions. And 657 of 1938 statements are stale — a third of the log.\n\nThose numbers are one mechanism, not three. Fifty-five live artifacts at one path means each is a successor the others never superseded, which is what the omitted-supersession count is counting; and every merge that states a dot artifact superseding the previous one propagates staleness through work that shares nothing with it but the path. The path is a global mutex, exactly as f35c3447 says.\n\nThe circularity is worth naming because it decides how this gets unstuck. f35c3447 is the repair for that defect, and f35c3447 is itself stale: both its bases, 4b56ec2e and 1e3c1cec, are stale, and its commitment is reported-and-stale with the work sitting at head c28a770a on task/merge-artifact-paths, still reachable and still unmerged. The defect has made its own repair hard to read on the board. That is not an argument for hurrying it — it is an argument for refiling that lineage on live bases before anything else, because a reviewer looking at a stale request and a stale report cannot tell whether the work is current.\n\nSo the order is narrower than \"f35c3447 must land first\". It is: refile the f35c3447 lineage on live bases, get the work at c28a770a reviewed and merged, then this succession rule becomes sound, and only then do the counts in this request's conditions mean what they say. I have not started that refiling; I am recording the measurement so whoever does has it, and so the third-of-the-log figure is on the record rather than in one session's memory.\n\nOne caution about my own numbers: 657 stale statements is a count of the current projection, not a claim that all of it traces to path dot. I did not attribute each one. What I can say is that the dot path is the only mechanism I found that propagates across unrelated work by construction rather than by a participant deciding something."}